      "text": "## 1. Induction Agents"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#007",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery, git-history:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery]",
        "canonical_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "current_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "id": "maxim:medicine:anesthesia-surgery",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "section": "medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nPROPOFOL (2,6-diisopropylphenol):\n  Mechanism: positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A (Cl- channel → hyperpolarization)\n    Also: activates glycine receptors; attenuates NMDA receptor\n  Formulation: lipid emulsion (intralipid) — soybean oil/egg lecithin; white milky appearance\n    Supports bacterial growth → use within 12 hrs; dispose unused portions\n  Pharmacokinetics: extremely lipophilic → rapid distribution to brain → fast induction\n    Redistribution: effect offset by redistribution to peripheral compartments (not metabolism)\n    Elimination: hepatic + extrahepatic glucuronidation; CYP2B6 minor\n    Context-sensitive half-time: predictable even for prolonged infusions → ICU sedation"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#008",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery, git-history:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery]",
        "canonical_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "current_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "id": "maxim:medicine:anesthesia-surgery",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "section": "medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Adverse:\n    PAIN ON INJECTION: burning in small veins; inject into large vein or add lignocaine\n    HYPOTENSION: most clinically significant → vasodilation + negative inotropy; fluid/vasopressor ready\n    Apnea: brief apnea on induction → airway management essential immediately\n    Propofol Infusion Syndrome (PRIS): rare; high-dose prolonged infusion → metabolic acidosis + rhabdomyolysis + cardiac failure; limit <4 mg/kg/hr for >48hrs; avoid in critically ill children\n    Not an analgesic: anti-emetic effects; reduce PONV"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#009",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery, git-history:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery]",
        "canonical_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "current_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "id": "maxim:medicine:anesthesia-surgery",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "section": "medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "KETAMINE:\n  Mechanism: NMDA receptor antagonist (non-competitive; channels stays open but K+ blocks)\n    Also: μ-opioid agonist (weak); blocks monoamine reuptake (catecholamines)\n  Unique: DISSOCIATIVE anesthetic — analgesia + amnesia without full loss of consciousness\n  Airway: maintains pharyngeal reflexes and airway tone (does NOT remove risk of aspiration)\n  Hemodynamics: sympathomimetic → ↑ HR, BP, CO → useful in trauma/hemodynamic compromise\n  Bronchodilation: useful in reactive airway disease / asthmatic intubation\n  Analgesic properties: sub-anesthetic ketamine infusion for chronic pain, opioid-sparing\n  NMDA role in pain: central sensitization; ketamine reduces opioid tolerance/hyperalgesia"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#010",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery, git-history:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery]",
        "canonical_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "current_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "id": "maxim:medicine:anesthesia-surgery",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "section": "medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Adverse:\n    Emergence phenomena: hallucinations, vivid dreams, delirium on emergence\n      Attenuated by co-administration of benzodiazepines (midazolam) or low-dose propofol\n    Tachycardia/hypertension: relative contraindication in uncontrolled hypertension, ICP elevation\n    ICP: classically taught as raising ICP → now controversial; safe in TBI with controlled ventilation"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#011",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery, git-history:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery]",
        "canonical_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "current_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "id": "maxim:medicine:anesthesia-surgery",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "section": "medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "ETOMIDATE:\n  Mechanism: GABA-A potentiation (imidazole ring binds at distinct site from propofol)\n  Hemodynamic stability: minimal effect on BP/HR → preferred in hemodynamically unstable patients\n  ADRENOCORTICAL SUPPRESSION: blocks 11-β-hydroxylase → cortisol synthesis ↓ for 4-8 hrs\n    Single induction dose: transient suppression (clinically significant in septic shock?)\n    Controversy: avoid in adrenal insufficiency; caution in critical illness; some anesthesiologists avoid\n  Myoclonus: involuntary movements on induction; does not indicate seizure\n  No analgesia; anti-emetic effect less than propofol"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#012",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery, git-history:medicine:09-anesthesia-surgery]",
        "canonical_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "current_path": "medicine/09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md",
        "id": "maxim:medicine:anesthesia-surgery",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "section": "medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THIOPENTAL (sodium thiopentone):\n  Historical: barbiturate; first IV induction agent\n  Still used: ICP management (cerebral metabolic suppression); RSI in obstetric anesthesia historically\n  Tissue necrosis: intra-arterial injection → catastrophic; must confirm IV placement\n  Context-sensitive accumulation: prolonged infusion → very long emergence\n  Largely replaced by propofol in routine use\n```"
    },
