        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH):\n  Triggered by: all volatile anesthetic agents + succinylcholine\n  Genetic: mutations in RYR1 (ryanodine receptor, skeletal muscle SR Ca2+ channel)\n  Mechanism: uncontrolled skeletal muscle Ca2+ release → sustained contraction + metabolic crisis\n  Signs: rapid temperature rise, CO2 production ↑, rigidity, metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis\n    LATE sign: temperature rise; EARLY: masseter spasm, unexplained ETCO2 rise, tachycardia\n  Treatment: DANTROLENE (inhibits RYR1 → stops Ca2+ release); 2.5 mg/kg IV STAT; repeat prn\n    Also: stop volatile agent, 100% O2, cool patient, treat acidosis, treat hyperkalemia\n  Susceptibility testing: caffeine-halothane contracture test (CHCT) on muscle biopsy\n  MH-safe anesthesia: propofol + opioid + non-depolarizing NMB + avoid succinylcholine"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#019",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
