        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "NITROUS OXIDE:\n  MAC 104% → cannot produce full anesthesia in air (max ~70% N2O in clinical use)\n  Use: adjunct; analgesic (\"laughing gas\" for dental); second gas effect with volatile agent\n  Mechanism: NMDA antagonism (like ketamine) + opioid modulation\n  Vitamin B12 oxidation: irreversible inactivation of B12-dependent methionine synthase\n    Homocysteine accumulates; methionine synthesis impaired; myelopathy with prolonged exposure\n    AVOID: in vitamin B12 deficiency, MTHFR polymorphism, bowel obstruction (gas expansion)\n  Bone marrow: megaloblastic changes with prolonged surgical exposure; monitor in prolonged cases\n  Environmental: global warming potential; anesthetic gas scavenging systems required\n```"
    },
