        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[anesthesia, surgery]",
        "section": "medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "09 - Anesthesia and Perioperative Care",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "ADVERSE EFFECTS:\n    Hyperkalemia: K+ released during fasciculations; safe normally (~0.5 mEq/L rise)\n      DANGEROUS hyperkalemia (5-10 mEq/L rise) in:\n        Burns (>24hr post-burn; upregulation of extrajunctional nAChRs for ~6 months)\n        Crush injuries, prolonged immobilization\n        Denervation injuries (spinal cord injury, stroke, UMN lesion)\n        Severe muscle disease (Duchenne: rhabdomyolysis risk)\n    Masseter spasm: after succinylcholine → may indicate MH susceptibility\n    Bradycardia: muscarinic stimulation (especially with second dose in children)\n    Increased ICP, IOP, intragastric pressure: theoretical; not proven clinically significant\n    Cannot be reversed: must wait for metabolism; rocuronium+sugammadex preferred if reversal needed immediately"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md#027",
      "path": [
        "09-ANESTHESIA-SURGERY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
